WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.000
Zoos have changed
out of all recognition.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.640
There are quite a lot of different
species around the planet,

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.440
which we wouldn't have today
if it wasn't for zoos.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:11.280
What do you think an ideal zoo
would look like?

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:12.480
Closed.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:20.960
All through history, we've seen
royal families,

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.960
powerful people, emperors,
have collections of animals.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.360
The king who was a good hunter
was probably a good warrior,

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:28.840
probably protect his people.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:31.600
Killing a lion or a bear
was a big deal,

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.800
but bringing back a live one
showed even more power.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.400
Virtually all the history of zoos
is about human control of nature.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:39.840
And it was a way of showing off.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:43.880
Queen Charlotte, her gift to everybody
that she liked

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.000
was to give them a kangaroo.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:52.120
And eventually we get the first
modern zoo opening in London in 1828.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:55.760
To be able to suddenly see
a giraffe for the first time,

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.560
probably it was absolutely bonkers.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:05.680
There wasn't really an appreciation
of animals needing certain things

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:07.160
to have a good life.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:11.120
So you would see them in barren
boxes, effectively,

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.760
just as you would see a specimen
in a natural history museum.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.040
JON COE: The story was, here's an object

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:18.800
divorced from habitat,
divorced from evolution.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.120
His name is, by the way,
knucklehead.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:22.200
Knucklehead?!

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:25.680
Well, as a rule, tapirs
are rather stupid and unintelligent.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:28.320
Animals were thought
of as basically automatons

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:30.640
just responding in a reflexive manner,

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:33.680
with no feelings of pain
or any kind of emotional depth.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.960
But that's really shifted.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.960
Now there's much
more of an understanding

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.800
that we really need to look
at their own type of intelligence

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.800
and how they live in their world.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.520
The schoolchildren that come
here to the wildlife park,

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:58.440
all of them go away learning
the messages that we know for sure

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.640
are going to make a true difference
to the animals

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:03.080
back out into the wild.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:06.480
The biggest threat to wildlife comes
from people,

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.080
and most people live in cities.

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:13.840
So zoos are very important because they
provide an educational opportunity

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:17.400
for people to learn about what the impacts
of their daily activities are

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:19.080
in far away places.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:24.000
JON COE: The zoo, by becoming a public
attraction, if it's done properly,

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:27.440
it seems to me like a pretty good idea.
The animals have a good life.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.360
People enjoy it. They learn something.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.240
Hopefully they get involved
in conservation.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:35.680
JON MINION: Pretty much all good zoos
throughout the world

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:41.240
are participators, or they help
to support and fund a whole host

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:44.320
of in-situ conservation programmes.

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:46.400
And if it wasn't for those programmes,

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:49.440
the world would be looking
very different right now.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.360
Zoo populations of animals are insurance
populations.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.440
Certain species,

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:08.200
due to what's going on on the planet
at the moment,

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:10.360
the only way that we can stop them
from going extinct

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:12.040
will be to bring them into captivity.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:14.880
As we learn to adapt to things
like climate change,

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:18.200
we are going to see an increasing
role for zoos.

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:25.200
You can visit zoos all over
the world that are just awful.

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:29.120
You know, they're as bad
as they were in the 1920s.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:32.400
ROBERT YOUNG: In the US they don't have
very strict animal welfare laws.

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:35.840
People can have animals in their back
garden and call it a zoo.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:39.840
There are more pet tigers in the US
than there are tigers in the wild.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:44.640
The legislation doesn't permit
that kind of thing here in the UK,

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:46.800
but you can get bad zoos anywhere.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:56.160
Those are all signs that they're missing
out on some important aspect

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:57.920
of how they live in the wild.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:02.120
The complexity of providing a good life
for some of these animals,

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:04.120
it's actually really, really difficult.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:12.680
There is some evidence that people
will know more facts

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.760
about a particular animal, for example,

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:18.600
but follow up studies to see if they've
actually changed their behaviour

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:21.600
to benefit conservation,

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:24.560
there's not much evidence
to suggest that is happening.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:29.200
The official statistics show that 4% of
revenue goes back into conservation.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:31.080
I mean, that's minimal. That's very small.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:33.960
And what do you find in zoos?

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.360
Big cats and elephants and zebra

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:37.840
and things that don't get released
back into the wild.

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:40.880
So if you want to save the condor,

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:43.760
then you can have an in situ
conservation programme

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:46.240
that's based in the country
where they come from.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.160
Most five year olds
are experts in dinosaurs.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:53.200
They've never met one or seen one.

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:55.280
The fascination's there,

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:57.880
and I think the same can be
harnessed with animals.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:01.360
There's many other technologies that are
being developed these days,

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:04.480
like awesome CGI, awesome robotics.

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:07.320
I think the future holds
something really cool

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:08.560
in the sense of learning about animals

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:11.840
without the detriment
of keeping them in captivity.

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:16.320
Virtual reality with wildlife
encounters is really coming on.

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:20.000
Taking a virtual walk through a herd of
elephants in the Serengeti,

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:23.320
I think, is going to be possible
pretty soon.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:26.800
Do you think zoos should exist?

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:31.280
It's kind of strange that
we transport all of these animals

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:34.680
from far from home
and keep them locked up

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:38.040
where you should see what nature
has to offer on your doorstep.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:42.320
Wildlife documentary, virtual reality -

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:46.560
none of these things have the same
positive emotional impact that a zoo has.

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:50.000
10% of the global population
visit zoos every year.

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:52.560
People from all walks of life
and all social classes.

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:55.440
So, I think, without zoos,
we'd lose a massive opportunity

00:05:55.480 --> 00:06:00.280
to keep people being interested in the
wild environment.

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:04.560
How can we create a world
where we don't need zoos?

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:09.440
What I'm hoping is that we can
design super sustainable cities

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:13.040
so that you don't need to go
to a place to see animals.

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:14.600
They're all around you.

